window.docContents[21].resolve({"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--From-conservation-laws-to-incompressible-flow--Axisymmetric-coordinates":{"contents":"Axial symmetry replaces the three-dimensional rotation group by rotations\naround one fixed axis. Scalar fields are invariant under these rotations,\nwhereas velocity fields are equivariant. In cylindrical variables the natural\nmeridian measure is , and a streamfunction reconstructs the radial\nand axial velocity components while enforcing incompressibility. The classical\nanalysis of  is an early systematic\nsource for this axisymmetric formulation.\n\nThe weight  has a geometric meaning: a meridian rectangle of area\n sweeps out a ring of volume . After integrating\nout the angle, conservation must still account for that growing circumference.\nThus incompressibility becomes\n, and the streamfunction enforces it\nby making the two terms opposite mixed derivatives.\n\nThe rotation action, meridian lifts, streamfunction identities, and the\nradial-four-plus-axial lift of the stream operator concern coordinates, not\nflows. The flows come in\nAxisymmetric Flow with Swirl, where profiles\nin the squared radius  give velocities that are smooth across the\naxis. The inverse direction, recovering the streamfunction from the vorticity,\nis formalized for an abstract stream operator with a supplied bounded inverse:\ncomposed with a bounded velocity reconstruction, it gives a Biot–Savart\noperator whose norm is at most the product of the two constants.\n\n\n\nRotation through  around the third coordinate axis preserves the\ncylindrical variables  and . Scalar fields\nmay therefore be represented by meridian profiles , while vector\nfields use the corresponding equivariance condition.\n\nThe rotations form an additive action,preserve  and , and every meridian lift  is\nrotation invariant.\n\nExpand the first two coordinates using the angle-addition formulas for sine\nand cosine. Their squared sum is unchanged, the third coordinate is fixed,\nand invariance of a meridian lift follows because its two inputs are\nunchanged.\n\nInvariance of  and  is what makes a meridian streamfunction\n a legitimate unknown: a swirl-free equivariant velocity field is\ndetermined by two meridian components, and in the measure  its\nincompressibility says that  is divergence-free in the\nhalf-plane, which is the form a rotated gradient of one scalar takes.\n\nFor a meridian streamfunction , we useDivision by  is totalized in Lean, so velocity and vorticity statements\nthat use it retain an explicit off-axis hypothesis .\n\nA  streamfunction generates conservative meridian flux with zero\ndivergence. Away from , the reconstructed velocities satisfy the two\ndisplayed flux identities, and for ,\n\nThe divergence cancellation is equality of mixed partial derivatives. The\nvelocity identities cancel the nonzero radius in the reconstruction formulas.\nFor the radial lift, apply the product rule twice to , substitute\ninto , and collect the radial terms.\n\nThe coefficient  identifies the new operator: a radial function in\nfour transverse dimensions has Laplacian\n. Adding the axial derivative gives a\nfive-dimensional Laplacian acting on this lifted profile. This is why the\nsubstitution  is useful for elliptic estimates. The identity\nholds off the axis; whether the lifted profile extends smoothly across \ndepends on the original streamfunction near the axis.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009From conservation laws to incompressible flow","header":"8.1.2. Axisymmetric coordinates","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--From-conservation-laws-to-incompressible-flow--Axisymmetric-coordinates"}});